2026-10-20T21:06:14.0611349Z +               '* Timur Yunusov, Alexey Osipov: [XML Out-Of-Band Data Retrieval](https://www.slideshare.net/qqlan/bh-ready-v4).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0613021Z +               '* Python 3 standard library: [XML Vulnerabilities](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0614928Z +               '* Python 2 standard library: [XML Vulnerabilities](https://docs.python.org/2/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0616447Z +               '* PortSwigger: [XML external entity (XXE) injection](https://portswigger.net/web-security/xxe).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0617805Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-611](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0619355Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-827](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/827.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0620302Z +             text: '# XML external entity expansion\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0624317Z +               'Parsing untrusted XML files with a weakly configured XML parser may lead to an XML External Entity (XXE) attack. This type of attack uses external entity references to access arbitrary files on a system, carry out denial-of-service (DoS) attacks, or server-side request forgery. Even when the result of parsing is not returned to the user, DoS attacks are still possible and out-of-band data retrieval techniques may allow attackers to steal sensitive data.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0627656Z -               '* [OWASP SSRF article](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Server_Side_Request_Forgery)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0628860Z -               '* [PortSwigger SSRF article](https://portswigger.net/web-security/ssrf)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0630117Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-918](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0631082Z -             text: '# Full server-side request forgery\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0635464Z -               'Directly incorporating user input into an HTTP request without validating the input can facilitate server-side request forgery (SSRF) attacks. In these attacks, the request may be changed, directed at a different server, or via a different protocol. This can allow the attacker to obtain sensitive information or perform actions with escalated privilege.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0637961Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0638804Z -               'We make a distinctions between how much of the URL an attacker can control:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0639552Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0640012Z +               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0643519Z +               'The easiest way to prevent XXE attacks is to disable external entity handling when parsing untrusted data. How this is done depends on the library being used. Note that some libraries, such as recent versions of the XML libraries in the standard library of Python 3, disable entity expansion by default, so unless you have explicitly enabled entity expansion, no further action needs to be taken.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0646745Z -               '* **Full SSRF**: where the full URL can be controlled.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0648167Z -               '* **Partial SSRF**: where only part of the URL can be controlled, such as the path component of a URL to a hardcoded domain.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0649180Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0650970Z +               'We recommend using the [defusedxml](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/) PyPI package, which has been created to prevent XML attacks (both XXE and XML bombs).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0652247Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0653439Z -               'Partial control of a URL is often much harder to exploit. Therefore we have created a separate query for each of these.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0655033Z                 '\n' +
