2026-10-20T21:06:14.0799749Z +             text: 'Disabling or weakening CSRF protection may make the application vulnerable to a Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attack.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0801417Z -             text: 'Writing user input directly to a web page allows for a cross-site scripting vulnerability.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0802202Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0802493Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0803100Z +             markdown: '# CSRF protection weakened or disabled\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0805009Z +               'Cross-site request forgery (CSRF) is a type of vulnerability in which an attacker is able to force a user to carry out an action that the user did not intend.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0806849Z -             markdown: '# Reflected server-side cross-site scripting\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0808829Z -               'Directly writing user input (for example, an HTTP request parameter) to a webpage without properly sanitizing the input first, allows for a cross-site scripting vulnerability.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0810230Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0814462Z +               "The attacker tricks an authenticated user into submitting a request to the web application. Typically this request will result in a state change on the server, such as changing the user's password. The request can be initiated when the user visits a site controlled by the attacker. If the web application relies only on cookies for authentication, or on other credentials that are automatically included in the request, then this request will appear as legitimate to the server.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0817589Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0820177Z +               'A common countermeasure for CSRF is to generate a unique token to be included in the HTML sent from the server to a user. This token can be used as a hidden field to be sent back with requests to the server, where the server can then check that the token is valid and associated with the relevant user session.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0822447Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0822788Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0823172Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0824858Z +               'In many web frameworks, CSRF protection is enabled by default. In these cases, using the default configuration is sufficient to guard against most CSRF attacks.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0828613Z -               'To guard against cross-site scripting, consider escaping the input before writing user input to the page. The standard library provides escaping functions: `html.escape()` for Python 3.2 upwards or `cgi.escape()` older versions of Python. Most frameworks also provide their own escaping functions, for example `flask.escape()`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0830886Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0831233Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0831628Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0833285Z +               'The following example shows a case where CSRF protection is disabled by overriding the default middleware stack and not including the one protecting against CSRF.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0837346Z -               'The following example is a minimal flask app which shows a safe and unsafe way to render the given name back to the page. The first view is unsafe as `first_name` is not escaped, leaving the page vulnerable to cross-site scripting attacks. The second view is safe as `first_name` is escaped, so it is not vulnerable to cross-site scripting attacks.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0839658Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0839990Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0840919Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0841419Z +               'MIDDLEWARE = [\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0842176Z +               "    'django.middleware.security.SecurityMiddleware',\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0843171Z +               "    'django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware',\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0844305Z +               "    'django.middleware.common.CommonMiddleware',\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0845181Z +               "    # 'django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware',\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0846142Z +               "    'django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware',\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0847189Z +               "    'django.contrib.messages.middleware.MessageMiddleware',\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0848245Z +               "    'django.middleware.clickjacking.XFrameOptionsMiddleware',\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0848948Z +               ']\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0849633Z -               'from flask import Flask, request, make_response, escape\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0850249Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0850614Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0852645Z +               'The protecting middleware was probably commented out during a testing phase, when server-side token generation was not set up. Simply commenting it back in will enable CSRF protection.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0854379Z -               'app = Flask(__name__)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0854850Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0855322Z -               "@app.route('/unsafe')\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0855879Z -               'def unsafe():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0856527Z -               "    first_name = request.args.get('name', '')\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0857376Z -               '    return make_response("Your name is " + first_name)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0857959Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0858396Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0859512Z +               '* Wikipedia: [Cross-site request forgery](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross-site_request_forgery)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0860962Z +               '* OWASP: [Cross-site request forgery](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/csrf)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.0862336Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-352](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html).\n',
