2026-10-20T21:06:14.1027435Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1027657Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1028228Z               text: 'Matching HTML tags using regular expressions is hard to do right, and can easily lead to security issues.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1028747Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1028934Z ...
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1029136Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1029733Z +             text: 'Making a network request to a URL that is fully user-controlled allows for request forgery attacks.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1030674Z -             text: 'Building a NoSQL query from user-controlled sources is vulnerable to insertion of malicious NoSQL code by the user.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1031235Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1031428Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1031757Z +             markdown: '# Full server-side request forgery\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1033565Z +               'Directly incorporating user input into an HTTP request without validating the input can facilitate server-side request forgery (SSRF) attacks. In these attacks, the request may be changed, directed at a different server, or via a different protocol. This can allow the attacker to obtain sensitive information or perform actions with escalated privilege.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1035476Z -             markdown: '# NoSQL Injection\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1037499Z -               'Passing user-controlled sources into NoSQL queries can result in a NoSQL injection flaw. This tainted NoSQL query containing a user-controlled source can then execute a malicious query in a NoSQL database such as MongoDB. In order for the user-controlled source to taint the NoSQL query, the user-controller source must be converted into a Python object using something like `json.loads` or `xmltodict.parse`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1039055Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1039525Z +               'We make a distinctions between how much of the URL an attacker can control:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1041236Z -               'Because a user-controlled source is passed into the query, the malicious user can have complete control over the query itself. When the tainted query is executed, the malicious user can commit malicious actions such as bypassing role restrictions or accessing and modifying restricted data in the NoSQL database.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1042444Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1042816Z +               '* **Full SSRF**: where the full URL can be controlled.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1043604Z +               '* **Partial SSRF**: where only part of the URL can be controlled, such as the path component of a URL to a hardcoded domain.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1044369Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1044587Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1045231Z +               'Partial control of a URL is often much harder to exploit. Therefore we have created a separate query for each of these.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1045802Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1046291Z +               'This query covers full SSRF, to find partial SSRF use the `py/partial-ssrf` query.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1046739Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1046952Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1047194Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1050315Z +               'To guard against SSRF attacks you should avoid putting user-provided input directly into a request URL. On the application level, maintain a list of authorized URLs on the server and choose from that list based on the input provided. If that is not possible, one should verify the IP address for all user-controlled requests to ensure they are not private. This requires saving the verified IP address of each domain, then utilizing a custom HTTP adapter to ensure that future requests to that domain use the verified IP address. On the network level, you can segment the vulnerable application into its own LAN or block access to specific devices.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1053985Z -               "NoSQL injections can be prevented by escaping user-input's special characters that are passed into the NoSQL query from the user-supplied source. Alternatively, using a sanitize library such as MongoSanitizer will ensure that user-supplied sources can not act as a malicious query.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1055158Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1055374Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1055610Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1057287Z +               'The following example shows code vulnerable to a full SSRF attack, because it uses untrusted input (HTTP request parameter) directly to construct a URL. By using `evil.com#` as the `target` value, the requested URL will be `https://evil.com#.example.com/data/`. It also shows how to remedy the problem by using the user input select a known fixed string.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1059011Z -               'In the example below, the user-supplied source is passed to a MongoDB function that queries the MongoDB database.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1059766Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1059983Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1060215Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1060510Z +               'import requests\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1060826Z                 'from flask import Flask, request\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1061217Z -               'from flask_pymongo import PyMongo\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1061550Z -               'import json\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1061801Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1062072Z +               'app = Flask(__name__)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1062406Z -               'mongo = PyMongo(app)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1062671Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1062958Z +               '@app.route("/full_ssrf")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1063288Z +               'def full_ssrf():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1063645Z +               '    target = request.args["target"]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1064168Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1064485Z +               '    # BAD: user has full control of URL\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1064992Z +               '    resp = requests.get("https://" + target + ".example.com/data/")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1065426Z -               '@app.route("/")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1065738Z -               'def home_page():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1066124Z -               "    unsanitized_search = request.args['search']\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1066578Z -               '    json_search = json.loads(unsanitized_search)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1066901Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1067252Z +               '    # GOOD: `subdomain` is controlled by the server.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1067747Z +               '    subdomain = "europe" if target == "EU" else "world"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1068327Z +               '    resp = requests.get("https://" + subdomain + ".example.com/data/")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1068877Z -               "    result = mongo.db.user.find({'name': json_search})\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1069227Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1069436Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1070076Z -               'This can be fixed by using a sanitizer library like MongoSanitizer as shown in this annotated code version below.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1070636Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1070888Z +               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1072834Z +               'The following example shows code vulnerable to a partial SSRF attack, because it uses untrusted input (HTTP request parameter) directly to construct a URL. By using `../transfer-funds-to/123?amount=456` as the `user_id` value, the requested URL will be `https://api.example.com/transfer-funds-to/123?amount=456`. It also shows how to remedy the problem by validating the input.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1074470Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1074692Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1074930Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1075225Z +               'import requests\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1075541Z                 'from flask import Flask, request\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1075924Z -               'from flask_pymongo import PyMongo\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1076357Z -               'from mongosanitizer.sanitizer import sanitize\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1076729Z -               'import json\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1076979Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1077246Z +               'app = Flask(__name__)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1077575Z -               'mongo = PyMongo(app)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1077840Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1078134Z +               '@app.route("/partial_ssrf")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1078478Z +               'def partial_ssrf():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1078855Z +               '    user_id = request.args["user_id"]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1079153Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1079539Z +               '    # BAD: user can fully control the path component of the URL\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1080290Z +               '    resp = requests.get("https://api.example.com/user_info/" + user_id)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1080739Z -               '@app.route("/")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1081056Z -               'def home_page():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1081425Z -               "    unsafe_search = request.args['search']\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1081851Z -               '    json_search = json.loads(unsafe_search)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1082286Z -               '    safe_search = sanitize(unsanitized_search)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1082600Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1082881Z +               '    if user_id.isalnum():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1083495Z +               '        # GOOD: user_id is restricted to be alpha-numeric, and cannot alter path component of URL\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1084479Z +               '        resp = requests.get("https://api.example.com/user_info/" + user_id)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1085100Z -               "    result = client.db.collection.find_one({'data': safe_search})\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1085487Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1085696Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1085916Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1086151Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1086722Z +               '* [OWASP SSRF article](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Server_Side_Request_Forgery)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1087439Z +               '* [PortSwigger SSRF article](https://portswigger.net/web-security/ssrf)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1088172Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-918](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1088752Z +             text: '# Full server-side request forgery\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1090568Z +               'Directly incorporating user input into an HTTP request without validating the input can facilitate server-side request forgery (SSRF) attacks. In these attacks, the request may be changed, directed at a different server, or via a different protocol. This can allow the attacker to obtain sensitive information or perform actions with escalated privilege.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1092115Z -               '* Mongoengine: [Documentation](http://mongoengine.org/).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1092883Z -               '* Flask-Mongoengine: [Documentation](http://docs.mongoengine.org/projects/flask-mongoengine/en/latest/).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1093629Z -               '* PyMongo: [Documentation](https://pypi.org/project/pymongo/).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1094550Z -               '* Flask-PyMongo: [Documentation](https://flask-pymongo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1095299Z -               '* OWASP: [NoSQL Injection](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/GOD16-NOSQL.pdf).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1096492Z -               '* Security Stack Exchange Discussion: [Question 83231](https://security.stackexchange.com/questions/83231/mongodb-nosql-injection-in-python-code).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1097511Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-943](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1098052Z -             text: '# NoSQL Injection\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1100054Z -               'Passing user-controlled sources into NoSQL queries can result in a NoSQL injection flaw. This tainted NoSQL query containing a user-controlled source can then execute a malicious query in a NoSQL database such as MongoDB. In order for the user-controlled source to taint the NoSQL query, the user-controller source must be converted into a Python object using something like `json.loads` or `xmltodict.parse`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1101603Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1102072Z +               'We make a distinctions between how much of the URL an attacker can control:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1103990Z -               'Because a user-controlled source is passed into the query, the malicious user can have complete control over the query itself. When the tainted query is executed, the malicious user can commit malicious actions such as bypassing role restrictions or accessing and modifying restricted data in the NoSQL database.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1105386Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1105764Z +               '* **Full SSRF**: where the full URL can be controlled.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1106550Z +               '* **Partial SSRF**: where only part of the URL can be controlled, such as the path component of a URL to a hardcoded domain.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1107109Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1107325Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1107972Z +               'Partial control of a URL is often much harder to exploit. Therefore we have created a separate query for each of these.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1108535Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1109050Z +               'This query covers full SSRF, to find partial SSRF use the `py/partial-ssrf` query.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1109503Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1109727Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1109972Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1112930Z +               'To guard against SSRF attacks you should avoid putting user-provided input directly into a request URL. On the application level, maintain a list of authorized URLs on the server and choose from that list based on the input provided. If that is not possible, one should verify the IP address for all user-controlled requests to ensure they are not private. This requires saving the verified IP address of each domain, then utilizing a custom HTTP adapter to ensure that future requests to that domain use the verified IP address. On the network level, you can segment the vulnerable application into its own LAN or block access to specific devices.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1116626Z -               "NoSQL injections can be prevented by escaping user-input's special characters that are passed into the NoSQL query from the user-supplied source. Alternatively, using a sanitize library such as MongoSanitizer will ensure that user-supplied sources can not act as a malicious query.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1117757Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1117961Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1118194Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1119868Z +               'The following example shows code vulnerable to a full SSRF attack, because it uses untrusted input (HTTP request parameter) directly to construct a URL. By using `evil.com#` as the `target` value, the requested URL will be `https://evil.com#.example.com/data/`. It also shows how to remedy the problem by using the user input select a known fixed string.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1121733Z -               'In the example below, the user-supplied source is passed to a MongoDB function that queries the MongoDB database.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1122281Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1122481Z                 '\n' +
